        answer:
          "The objection: Cicero writes in 44 BC, two and a half centuries before Diogenes and Sextus; his source is a Hellenistic handbook; he gives seven modes, as does Martianus Capella (4.414 to 421) from a related tradition; the convergence of two Latin witnesses on seven against two Greek witnesses on five is not obviously to be resolved for the Greek; and DL 7.79 itself says authorities differed on the number. Reply: (1) DL 7.79 and Sextus M 8.223 both attribute five to Chrysippus by name; Cicero attributes his seven to no one and is writing a manual of argument-finding for the jurist Trebatius, in which logical exactness is not the aim; (2) Cicero's sixth and seventh, built on the negated conjunction with a denied conjunct ('not both p and q; not p; therefore q' in the sixth), are invalid for the Stoic negated conjunction, which is true whenever at least one conjunct is false (both false satisfies it, and then q is false), so they cannot be Chrysippus's unless his leading premiss there was something other than a negated conjunction, for which there is no evidence; Frede (1974) draws this conclusion (Bobzien 1996 does not discuss the sixth and seventh); (3) 'authorities differed' at DL 7.79 is compatible with other Stoics having fewer or more, and the natural reading of the context is that Chrysippus's five was the school's settled number; (4) the Latin tradition also differs from the Greek in order and in the treatment of disjunction, which argues for a separate, rhetorical line of transmission rather than better access to Chrysippus. What the reply cannot do: show where the sixth and seventh came from. Ierodiakonou's work on the later lists is the place to see how little that question can be answered. The evidential conclusion: five for Chrysippus is as secure as anything in this logic; seven is a fact about a different tradition.",
