        answer:
          "Establishes: (i) a version of the third thema in Greek: when from two a third is concluded, and externals are taken that syllogize one of the two, then the same thing is concluded from the remaining one and the externals that syllogize the other (Bobzien 1996, p. 145 and n. 22); (ii) that the third thema is a chaining rule, in which external assumptions (exōthen) deduce one premiss of an argument, so that its conclusion follows from the other premiss and the externals, yielding an argument of three or more premisses; (iii) that Alexander, a Peripatetic of c. 200 AD, took it to say what Aristotle says at An. Pr. 41b36 to 42a19 about syllogisms with more than two premisses, which is itself evidence that the rule concerned the composition of longer syllogisms. Cannot establish: (a) that the externals deduced a premiss rather than joining a conclusion: the context is Aristotle's account of compound syllogisms from the side of synthesis, and Alexander may have restructured the Stoic rule to match (Bobzien 1996, pp. 145 to 151); Simplicius, in De Caelo 237.2 to 4, has a single external joining the conclusion of the first argument; (b) that the rule was used by the Stoics from the side of synthesis, since Alexander's purpose is to assimilate it to Aristotle's synthesis; the Stoic use was analysis (DL 7.78: anagomenoi, 'reduced'); (c) the meaning of exōthen: not among the two premisses, or not used; (d) anything about the second or fourth themata beyond what 284.10 to 17 and 164.27 to 31 add. Problems: Alexander is hostile to the themata (164.27 to 31, the second is useless) and to Stoic logic generally; his quotation is in the service of showing the Stoics had nothing Aristotle lacked; the text at 278 is in a commentary whose manuscripts Wallies edited from a tradition with known lacunae in this region. The ledger notes that some Alexander readings rest on emendation (FINDINGS.md section 6); check the apparatus at 278.12 to 14 before citing a single word of it as secure.",
