        answer:
          "Analysis. 'if p, (if p, q); p' form A1 with conclusion 'if p, q'; remainder 'if p, q; p; if q, r ⊢ r', which reuses p (a premiss of the split-off indemonstrable) and takes 'if q, r' from outside: T4. The remainder: 'if p, q; p' form A1 with conclusion q; remainder 'q; if q, r ⊢ r', A1 with external 'if q, r': T3. Forward. L1: if p, (if p, q); p ⊢ if p, q (A1). L2: if p, q; p ⊢ q (A1). L3: if q, r; q ⊢ r (A1). L4 (T3 on L2 and L3, L3 read as 'q; if q, r ⊢ r', E = 'if q, r'): if p, q; p; if q, r ⊢ r. L5 (T4 on L1 and L4, L4 read as 'if p, q; p; if q, r ⊢ r', Ai = p reused, E = 'if q, r'): if p, (if p, q); p; if q, r ⊢ r. The thema at L5 is T4, because the second argument uses both a premiss of the first (p) and an external ('if q, r'). T2 would not do (it allows no external), T3 would not do (it allows no reuse; without reuse the remainder 'if p, q; if q, r ⊢ r' is invalid). The argument is three-premiss and so within reach of the basic single-external third thema at L4, but L5 needs the fourth, which no text states; on Frede's system the same argument is reached by chaining to 'if p, (if p, q); p; p; if q, r ⊢ r' and contracting. This is the simplest argument that separates a reconstruction with T4 from one with T2 and T3 only, and a good test item for a Bobzien candidate in the Themata matrix.",
