        answer:
          "Establishes: (i) the division of conclusive arguments into syllogistic and 'conclusive in the specific sense' (the latter illustrated by 'it is false that it is both day and night; it is day; therefore it is not night', ledger S008); (ii) the definition of syllogistic: those that are indemonstrable, or reduced to the indemonstrables by one or more of the themata (ἀναγόμενοι ἐπὶ τοὺς ἀναποδείκτους κατά τι τῶν θεμάτων ἤ τινα); (iii) that reduction (anagein, analysis) runs from the syllogism back to the indemonstrables, which fixes the direction the themata were used in; (iv) that a reduction may use several themata (tina, plural), so the themata were applied in sequence or in combination; (v) the example of a syllogistic argument, 'if Dion walks, Dion is in motion; but Dion is walking; therefore Dion is in motion' (a first indemonstrable, so the example illustrates the first disjunct of the definition only); (vi) the word thema as the Stoic term, and that there was a plurality of them (the number four is from Galen and Alexander). Cannot establish: (a) the content of any thema; (b) the number; (c) whether the specific-sense arguments were held to be irreducible in principle or merely not syllogistic in form; (d) whether 'valid' (perantikos) was defined by the criterion of PH 2.137 (Diogenes gives a different formulation at 7.77, in terms of the contradictory of the conclusion conflicting with the conjunction of the premisses, which is closer to Chrysippus's conditional); (e) any completeness claim stronger than the definition. Problem in Hicks: 'in respect of one or more of the premisses' renders thematōn as if it were lēmmatōn; the Greek says 'themata', and a student relying on Hicks here (as the ledger's S008 passage does) would miss that the sentence names the reduction rules at all. Problems of the witness: Diogenes is a third-century compiler abridging a handbook; the section is compressed; the passage is the only place he names the themata.",
