          "8. Objection and reply. Frede (1974) holds that the second thema is a contraction rule removing a repeated premiss, not a chaining rule. State Bobzien's objections and give Frede's best reply, then say which text decides, if any.",
        answer:
          "Bobzien's objections (1996, section 3, nn. 45 and 50): (1) a contraction rule cannot reduce any argument on its own, since its output is its input with a premiss deleted; but a thema is, by DL 7.78, something by which a syllogism is reduced to indemonstrables; so Frede's second is not a thema in the attested sense; (2) Frede's third and fourth together do the work of her single third, so his system is redundant in one place to pay for a rule that does nothing in another; (3) Alexander 284.10 to 17 groups the second with the third and fourth as what the synthetic theorem does, and the synthetic theorem is a chaining rule; a deletion rule is not. Frede's reply: (1) DL 7.78 says syllogisms are reduced 'by one or more of the themata'; nothing says each thema reduces by itself, and a set of rules may include a structural rule that the others need; the second thema is needed because the chaining rules, stated as Alexander states them, produce repeated premisses whenever a premiss serves twice (Sextus's own example at M 8.232, where 'it is day' is used twice), and a system without weakening must say what to do with the repetition; (2) Alexander's 'useless' (164.27 to 31) is exactly what a Peripatetic would say of a bookkeeping rule, and is harder to explain of a chaining rule that does real reductive work; (3) 284 says the synthetic theorem does the work of the second to fourth together, which is consistent with the second contributing the contraction the theorem has built in (the Peripatetic theorem does not repeat premisses because it never counts them). Which text decides: none. 164 favours Frede slightly; 284 favours Bobzien slightly; DL 7.78 is neutral; the surviving third thema is consistent with both. The Themata project cannot decide it either: it reports that no attested argument in the ledger separates a second or fourth thema from what the first thema and a cut already give (FINDINGS.md section 5), and that every fitting candidate needs contraction, which both reconstructions supply (Bobzien within T2, Frede by his second). A student writing on this should say that the choice is between two ways of supplying contraction and that the sources name the rule without describing it.",
